speechtext:
Therefore, the thing is more serious than we thought. The seriousness of it has been growing, and we shall be lucky if it does not get worse. It is remarkable that under this adverse condition of affairs the apple industry of British Columbia in four years multiplied itself by four. Our contribution on the prairies to encourage an infant industry may be made in a spirit of charity, but I will be dashed if we want to help people who quadruple their wealth in four years -we are not doing that well ourselves. I have not heard the minister say: We will take something out of other parts of Canada and give it to the poor homesteaders in Saskatchewan, because they are not increasing their wealth 100 per cent every year. But if you apply that principle to British Columbia, why not apply it to Saskatchewan as well? My view of this whole protection business-I do not know whether any one cares about it or not;
I do not know whether it is worth five cents or not-is that if this Parliament wants to make pets and favourites of the apple growers of Br'tish Columbia, it ought to be done at the expense of the people of Canada, not at the expense of Saskatchewan and Alberta only. If the minister wants to help the apple growers of British Columbia, why does he not vote some money, fifty cents a barrel if he likes, check it up carefully and pay it out to these people? Let the people in the Annapolis valley pay a share of it; let the people of Quebec pay a share of it; let the whole people of Canada pay a share of it. That would be

more like a national policy; that would be putting the expense upon the nation as a . whole, instead of piling it all on the Saskatchewan and Alberta prairies. These prairie districts are a mark, not a market.

It would be necessary to find a market for the surplus apples that-are produced in Nova Scotia, but last year and the year before there were between
300,000	and 400,000 barrels imported.
]790
